\description{
A profile tells \code{\link[=anon_deidentify]{anon_deidentify()}} what each variable is: a direct
identifier to remove, the subject key and related IDs, a date to offset, a
quasi-identifier with its generalization ladder, a variable derived from a
quasi-identifier, or a variable to keep. \code{anon_profile_sdtm()} and
\code{anon_profile_adam()} describe standard CDISC SDTM and ADaM variables;
\code{anon_profile()} and \code{anon_profile_rule()} build a profile for other data,
or rules to add to a standard one with \code{\link[=anon_profile_combine]{anon_profile_combine()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{Structure.} A profile is a tibble of rules with class
\code{"anon_profile"} and columns \code{variable}, \code{datasets} (a list of character
vectors, empty for every dataset), \code{role}, \code{quasi_name}, \code{test_col},
\code{test}, \code{flag}, \code{ladder} (a list of lists of rules), \code{from} (a list of
character vectors), \code{priority} and \code{profile} (the name of the profile the
rule came from). \code{as.data.frame()} returns it as a data frame to edit, and
\code{anon_profile()} turns an edited data frame back into a profile.

\strong{Which rule applies.} For a column of a dataset, among the rules (other
than test rules) whose \code{variable} and \code{datasets} match it, a rule limited
to datasets wins over one that isn't, an exact name over a pattern, and a
later rule over an earlier one. Dataset names and patterns are matched
ignoring case. Test rules apply to the rows of their test in every dataset
(in \code{datasets}) with the test and result columns.

\strong{Companion variables.} ADaM names a variable's numeric code \code{XN} and its
decoded or long label \code{XL} or \code{XC} (\code{SITEIDN}, \code{COUNTRYL}, \code{USUBJIDN}).
\code{\link[=anon_deidentify]{anon_deidentify()}} gives the companions of the key and of each \code{"also"}
column the \code{"also"} role (a numeric companion gets the number of the
subject's pseudonym, 31 for \code{SUBJ-031}, the same in every dataset), and
makes the companions of each quasi-identifier column \code{"derived"} from it.
A rule that matches a companion's name wins.

Columns matched by no rule are kept as they are, except that dates are
offset and subject IDs replaced wherever \code{\link[=anon_subject_dates]{anon_subject_dates()}} and
\code{\link[=anon_subject_ids]{anon_subject_ids()}} find them. \code{\link[=anon_deidentify]{anon_deidentify()}} warns about columns
with the key whose names look like an age, a birth date, a site, a
numeric ID (\verb{*IDN}), a country, a race (\code{RACE1}), a height, a weight, a
BMI, a body surface area, a creatinine clearance or an eGFR (units, named
\verb{*U}, aside) and that no rule matches, unless they hold one value in every
row; such a column is a reason the target is not met. It also warns about
\code{"keep"} rules that win over a quasi-identifier's rule for a column.

\strong{Dates.} \code{\link[=anon_deidentify]{anon_deidentify()}} offsets dates with
\code{\link[=anon_subject_dates]{anon_subject_dates()}}'s own detection (every Date and date-time column
and every text column whose name ends in \code{DTC}, at any depth), and, with
the same offsets, the text columns of ISO 8601 dates matched by a
\code{"date"} rule (such as \code{VISIT_DATE}). A column matched by a \code{"date"} rule
that can't be offset (a numeric SAS date, say) is named in a warning.

\strong{\code{anon_profile_sdtm()}.}
\itemize{
\item remove: \code{BRTHDTC} (with \code{AGE} and an offset \code{RFSTDTC}, a birth date
gives the age back exactly, even after offsetting, and a birth year
undoes \code{AGE} bands, so it is removed rather than reduced to the year),
\code{INVNAM}, \code{INVID};
\item key \code{USUBJID}, also \code{SUBJID}, key_cols \code{RSUBJID};
\item dates: \code{--DTC}, \code{--STDTC}, \code{--ENDTC} and every other \verb{*DTC};
\item quasi-identifiers, each with its ladder: \code{AGE} (5-, 10- and 20-year
bands, ages of 90 and over as 90), \code{SEX} (suppressed only), \code{RACE}
(categories of fewer than \code{min_n} subjects folded into \code{"OTHER"}, a
CDISC controlled term), \code{ETHNIC} (suppressed only: its controlled
terminology has no neutral other value), \code{SITEID} (small sites merged
into \code{"OTHER"}, then removed), \code{COUNTRY} (removed: ISO 3166 has no code
for "other", so a small country can't be folded), the race qualifiers of
\code{SUPPDM} (\code{QVAL} where \code{QNAM} is \verb{RACE*}: copies of \code{RACE}, folded
alike), and baseline \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT} from \code{vs}
(\code{VSSTRESN} with \code{VSORRES} and \code{VSSTRESC}; flag \code{VSBLFL}, else the first
record: height is often recorded once, at screening, without the flag),
rounded to units, then to tens, then in bands of 20;
\item derived: \code{BMI} and \code{BSA} results in \code{vs}, from \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT}
(blanked where those changed);
\item duration: \code{RFSTDTC} to \code{RFENDTC}, in bands of 7 days;
\item keep: \code{AGEU} (the unit of \code{AGE}, not identifying), \code{--DY}, \code{--STDY} and
\code{--ENDY} (study days, which offsetting keeps exact).
}

Priorities (ties are broken by blanking first): \code{SITEID}, \code{COUNTRY},
\code{ETHNIC}, \code{RACE}, \code{HEIGHT}, \code{WEIGHT}, \code{AGE}, \code{SEX}.

The \code{AGE} ladder doesn't follow a study's age groups (such as \code{AGEGR1}
cut at 65): where a band straddles a cut, the group is blanked. To keep
them, combine the profile with an \code{AGE} rule whose ladder uses bands that
align with the cuts, such as \code{anon_band(5, bottom = 15)} for cuts at 65
(\code{AAGE} in ADaM takes its own rule).

\strong{\code{anon_profile_adam()}.} Rules match ADSL variables in every dataset
that carries them (ADSL, BDS and OCCDS datasets alike).
\itemize{
\item remove: \code{BRTHDT}, \code{BRTHDTM}, \code{BRTHDTC}, \code{INVNAM}, \code{INVID};
\item key, also and key_cols as for SDTM;
\item dates: \verb{*DT}, \verb{*DTM} and \verb{*DTC} (such as \code{TRTSDT}, \code{TRTEDT}, \code{RANDDT},
\code{ADT}, \code{ADTM});
\item quasi-identifiers: \code{AGE} (with \code{AAGE}, the age at an event, as a copy),
\code{SEX}, \code{RACE}, \code{ETHNIC}, \code{SITEID} and \code{COUNTRY} as for SDTM; \code{EDUCLVL}
(suppressed only); \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT} as copies in
\code{HEIGHTBL}/\code{HTBL}/\code{HGTBL} and \code{WEIGHTBL}/\code{WTBL}/\code{WGTBL}, in the \code{AVAL} of their \code{PARAMCD}
rows (flag \code{ABLFL}; \code{AVALC}, \code{BASE}, \code{BASEC}, \code{CHG}, \code{PCHG} and \code{R2BASE}
follow, see \code{\link[=anon_rule]{anon_rule()}}) and in the SDTM results (\code{VSSTRESN}) that BDS
datasets carry, with the SDTM ladders;
\item derived: \code{RACEN}, \verb{RACEGR*} from \code{RACE}; \code{SEXN} from \code{SEX}; \code{ETHNICN}
from \code{ETHNIC}; \verb{AGEGR*} (such as \code{AGEGR1} and \code{AGEGR1N}) from \code{AGE};
\verb{AAGEGR*} from \code{AAGE}; \verb{REGION*} from \code{COUNTRY}; \verb{SITEGR*} from
\code{SITEID}; \code{BMIBL} and \verb{BMIBLGR*} from \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT}; \verb{BSA*}
(such as \code{BSABL}) from \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT}, and \verb{CRCL*} and \verb{EGFR*}
(\code{CRCLBL}, \code{EGFRBL}) from \code{HEIGHT}, \code{WEIGHT}, \code{AGE} and \code{SEX}, their
units (\verb{*U}) kept; \verb{AVALCAT*}
and \code{AVALCA*N} of the \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT} rows from them; and the
\code{BMI} and \code{BSA} parameters, from \code{HEIGHT} and \code{WEIGHT};
\item duration: \code{TRTSDT} to \code{TRTEDT}, in bands of 7 days.
}

Columns that hold a duration exactly, such as \code{TRTDURD}, \code{ADURN} or
\code{--DUR}, and study days (\code{ADY}, \code{--DY}) are kept: offsetting doesn't change
them, and they can single subjects out like the durations measured among
the residual signals. Remove them if that matters.

In BDS datasets, results (\code{AVAL}, \code{AVALC}, \code{BASE}, \code{BASEC}, \code{CHG}, \code{PCHG},
\code{R2BASE}) are not quasi-identifiers, except for the baseline height and
weight parameters: a lab value or a blood pressure is not something an
attacker would know. Add rules for other parameters that are.

\strong{Combining.} \code{\link[=anon_profile_combine]{anon_profile_combine()}} (or \code{c()}) joins profiles, such as
the SDTM and ADaM profiles for a list holding both. A rule of a later
profile replaces a rule of an earlier one with the same \code{variable},
\code{datasets}, \code{test_col} and \code{test} (in its place); a replacement with
another role is named in a warning. Quasi-identifiers with the same name
are one quasi-identifier: one set of generalizations, applied to every
copy.
}
\examples{
anon_profile_sdtm()

# A profile for data that isn't CDISC: a key, a date and two
# quasi-identifiers.
anon_profile(
  anon_profile_rule("ID", "key"),
  anon_profile_rule("VISIT_DATE", "date"),
  anon_profile_rule("AGE", "quasi", ladder = list(anon_band(10, top = 90))),
  anon_profile_rule("SEX", "quasi"),
  name = "registry"
)

# The SDTM and ADaM profiles together, for a list holding both.
c(anon_profile_sdtm(), anon_profile_adam())

}
